>> title: Sauerkraut
>> category: Toppings & Pickles
>> tags: cabbage, fermented, no-cook, condiment, deli
>> counters: Deli, The Bowl Shop
>> aka: kraut, sour cabbage, kapusta kiszona, kiszona kapusta, sauerkraut for reubens, fermented cabbage
>> pairs-with: corned-beef, sour-dill-pickles, deli-rye-bread
>> servings: 12
>> time: 23 days 30 min
>> slack: forgiving — nothing may sit above the brine: what is submerged ferments and what is exposed goes mouldy

>> step: shred the cabbage, salt at 2%
Shred @green cabbage{2%kg}(4 1/2 lb; two large heads, cored, sliced as thin as a coin) and toss it hard with @kosher salt{40%g}(2 1/2 Tbs Diamond Crystal) and @caraway seed{1%Tbs}.

>> step: press 30 min until it runs
Press @&(~1)salted cabbage{} down in the bowl with your fists or a weight and ~press{30%min}, until it has slumped by half and the liquid covers it.

>> step: pack under its own brine
Pack @&(~1)wilted cabbage{} into a #half-gallon jar{} a handful at a time, pressing each layer under the liquid, and top up if needed with @water{1%cup}(240 mL) mixed with @kosher salt{1%tsp}.

>> step: ferment 3 weeks at 65°F (18°C)
Weight @&(~1)packed jar{} so everything stays below the surface, cover it loosely so the gas can escape, and ~ferment{3%weeks} at 65°F (18°C), out of the sun.

>> step: refrigerate 2 days
Refrigerate @&(~1)sauerkraut{} and ~chill{2%days} before eating, which settles the sourness.
